    <h2>Insight #1 - Insights Mini-Paper</h2>
    <p>Throughout my internship, I learned that effective leadership is about asking the right questions, but not knowing the answers. Initially, I believed that project managers needed to supervise all project components and provide solutions, but I was shocked to see that my boss pushed us to think critically by promoting team discussions and asking open-ended questions instead of giving us tasks. This experience fundamentally changed my perspective on leadership by showing me that leading a group through inquiry fosters creativity and participation more than simply issuing commands.</p>
    <p>Another significant insight I gained was that confidence is not innate but rather is acquired through preparation and experience. I was afraid to speak out in meetings early in my career because I believed that only the most experienced individuals should do so. However, I found that after investing time in preparation and research, I could articulate my ideas well and participate in discussions. Because of this mental change, I now approach problems differently; I no longer see confidence as something that someone either has, but rather as something that can be gained through effort.</p>
    <p>Now, I've learned that being flexible means more than just responding to change; it also means seeing uncertainty as an opportunity for growth. My internship project required me to learn a new software program on my own with little guidance at the last minute. I sought resources to help me teach myself and, instead of becoming worried, I reframed the situation as a chance to acquire a new ability. This experience altered the way I see unforeseen challenges by teaching me that uncertainty may be a driving force for learning and development rather than a reversal.</p>
